#!/bin/bash
# claude-code/detect — always exits 0.
#
# Claude Code is the one harness every Permanence install already has, by construction — you
# need some AI tool with shell access just to run install.sh in the first place
# (design/harness-binding-mechanism.md §2). Unlike Antigravity or any other optional binding,
# there's nothing meaningful to gate: a heuristic check ("does ~/.claude already exist?", "is a
# claude binary on PATH?") is circular on a genuinely fresh install, since claude-code/wire
# itself is what creates ~/.claude in the first place, and many real users drive Claude Code
# through the desktop app or an IDE extension with no CLI binary on PATH at all. Confirmed the
# hard way: a real CI run had neither signal present and the whole binding silently, correctly
# (by its own broken logic) never ran — install.sh reported "done." with nothing actually wired.
exit 0
